The order observations generalize to the two classic theorems, both of them one-line corollaries of the group structure once Lagrange lands. Euler: for any unit $a$ mod $n$, $a^(phi(n)) equiv 1 mod n$, pinned here as $3^4 = 81 equiv 1 mod 10$ and $5^4 = 625 equiv 1 mod 12$. Fermat: for prime $p$ and $a != 0$, $a^(p - 1) equiv 1 mod p$, since $U(p)$ is all of ${1, ..., p - 1}$ and $phi(p) = p - 1$. The prime fixture is $3^100 equiv 1 mod 101$, sharpened to the exact statement that 3 generates all of $U(101)$: its order is 100, with $3^50 = 100 equiv -1 mod 101$ sitting at the halfway point.

