== order, cosets, and lagrange

The theorem this chapter exists for. For a subgroup $H$ of a finite group $G$, the left cosets are the translates $g H = {g dot h : h in H}$, and Lagrange's theorem states the partition arithmetic: $|G| = [G : H] dot |H|$, where $[G : H]$ is the number of distinct cosets (Wikipedia, "Lagrange's theorem (group theory)", fetched 2026-09-21: "if H is a subgroup of any finite group G, then |H| is a divisor of |G|. That is, the order (number of elements) of every subgroup divides the order of the whole group"). The proof is three short steps and worth doing, because it is the model for every counting argument in #xref-to("math", "actions").

+ Every coset has size exactly $|H|$: the map $h arrow.r g dot h$ is a bijection $H -> g H$, with inverse $g h arrow.r h$ computed by multiplying on the left by $g^(-1)$.
+ Distinct cosets are disjoint: if $g_1 H ∩ g_2 H$ is nonempty, pick $g_1 h_1 = g_2 h_2$, then $g_1 = g_2 h_2 h_1^(-1)$ forces $g_1 H = g_2 H$. So the cosets "form a partition of G", equal-size classes of the equivalence relation $x ~ y$ iff $x = y h$ for some $h in H$.
+ Count the partition: $|G|$ elements split into $[G : H]$ classes of $|H|$ each, so $|G| = [G : H] dot |H|$ and $|H|$ divides $|G|$.

The dry run: the additive cosets of $⟨3⟩ = {0, 3, 6, 9}$ inside $Z_12$, translation instead of multiplication because the group is additive.

+ $0 + ⟨3⟩ = {0, 3, 6, 9}$, the subgroup itself.
+ $1 + ⟨3⟩ = {1, 4, 7, 10}$ and $2 + ⟨3⟩ = {2, 5, 8, 11}$, two fresh sets.
+ $3 + ⟨3⟩$ lands back on the first coset, so the run stops: exactly 3 cosets of size 4, tiling all 12 residues with no overlap, $12 = 3 dot 4$.

