The corollary with teeth: since $⟨g⟩$ is a subgroup for every element, $"ord"(g) = |⟨g⟩|$ divides $|G|$, and consequently $g^(|G|) = e$ for every element of a finite group (the Lagrange page again: "If the group has n elements, it follows $a^n = e$"). Euler and Fermat from the second section are this corollary in disguise: $U(n)$ has $phi(n)$ elements, so every unit satisfies $a^(phi(n)) = 1$, and prime moduli give $a^(p - 1) = 1$. Gauss proved exactly that case, the multiplicative group mod a prime, in the Disquisitiones Arithmeticae of 1801, decades before the general theorem.

